  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry weather prevails through Sunday with the \nexception of a few scattered showers Thursday. Highs consistently \nin the 60s after a warmer Thursday. Saturday morning may be frosty \nin spots. \n\nHigh pressure dominates during this stretch of weather once we get \npast today as a pre-frontal trough and a moisture starved surface \ncold front coming swinging through this morning. Just enough \nmoisture to squeeze out some isolated showers, mainly in the early \nmorning (west) and late morning/early afternoon (east). Kept only a \nslight chance of showers at most, though many will stay dry. After a \nperiod of increased cloudcover with the frontal passage clear skies \nreturn in the afternoon and overnight. As sprawling high pressure \nexpands overhead this will lead to cool and dry weather through much \nof if not all of Sunday. Cool NW flow around the high causes 925mb \ntemps to drop from 15C today to 8-10C Fri/Sat leading high temps to \nstruggle to escape the low to mid 60s. The airmass moderates Sunday \nas return flow kicks in behind the exiting high. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next quick moving round of rain comes Sunday \nevening and overnight, exiting sometime on Monday though confidence \nin exact timing is low. Not expecting any flooding or damaging winds \nat this time. \n\nConfidence remains moderate to high that our next rain event arrives \nlate Sunday/Sunday night and is quite progressive as the remnants of \nTropical Storm Isaias are swept up in a northern stream trough. This \nushers it through rather quickly before deepening over the Gulf of \nMaine. While moisture parameters are good, at least over southern \nparts of the area, the progressive nature of the system will limit \nrainfall amounts and mitigate flooding issues. On the whole this \nlooks to be a fairly typical rain maker for the region without \nhazards from flooding or damaging winds. Guidance generally paints a \npicture of a quarter inch or less for northern MA to 1.25\" along the \nsouth shore in closer proximity to the better forcing and moisture. \nEnsemble probabilities of >1\" of rain are essentially NIL except for \n10-20% along the immediate south coast.",
